# Test: a plugin that declares fatal and exits stops ze
#
# The plugin is a REAL external process. It completes the handshake, says it is
# leaving, and returns. Nothing asks the daemon to stop, so the daemon stopping
# is the whole assertion: it read what the plugin declared and acted on it.
#
# `fatal` is open to any plugin, ze-shipped or operator-written (owner
# directive, 2026-09-06): configuring a plugin is accepting its terms.
#
# VALIDATES: AC-3 -- a fatal plugin's exit stops the daemon, and the log names
# the plugin and the reason.
# PREVENTS: fatal being implemented as a log line. The daemon runs until
# something ends the wait in waitLoop (cmd/ze/hub/main.go), so a policy that only
# logs leaves the router up without the plugin its operator was told it could not
# run without.

tmpfs=ze-bgp.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external failure-fatal {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/failure-policy-fatal"
		encoder json
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze start ze-bgp.conf:timeout=30s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=FIXTURE: failure-fatal is exiting
expect=stderr:contains=stopping ze because a plugin failed
expect=stderr:contains=the plugin declares that its failure stops ze
